  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday: The offshore low will spiral down the\ncoast, ejecting shortwaves across the Inland NW before it too \npivots inland tonight into Friday. One of those shortwave began \nits trek inland last night and will be moving tracking northeast\nthis morning. Rain along and ahead of it will impact the \nnortheast half of WA and the ID Panhandle early this morning, \nshifting to mainly the northern counties and Idaho Panhandle \nlate this morning. Thereafter the precipitation start to turn \nmore isolated to scattered in nature, becoming more convective \nfocused around the mountains and up against the Cascades for \nthis afternoon. Some embedded t-storms are possible this morning\nalong the axis of that shortwave and then in more isolated \nfashion around the mountains this afternoon. Heading into this \nevening, tonight and Friday that low pivots in. The showers and \nt-storm threat near the Cascades wane through dark, but farther \neast shortwave moves up the northern Rockies. That will renew \nrain chances over southeast WA/lower ID this evening and across \nnortheast WA to northern ID late this evening into the \novernight, wrapping back toward the norther mountains through \nFriday morning. By Friday afternoon the linger upper low and \nboundary near the SE CWA will continue to mean some risk for \nshowers and t-storm. Chances will be best around the mountains \nand others will be possible in SE WA and the lower ID Panhandle \nnear that boundary and some of the better lapse rates and \ninstability. \n\nWind will remain a bit breezy today, especially over central WA \nwith winds from the south to southwest at 10 to 15 mph and gusts \nup to 20 mph. However comparatively lighter winds are forecast \nfor Friday. Highs will be in the 60s to lower 70s. Lows will in \nthe upper 30s and 40s Thursday night and mid-30s to 40s Friday \nnight/Saturday AM. We will have to watch for some patchy frost \nthen.\n\nSaturday to Wednesday: The Inland NW remains dominated by the\ntrough of low pressure and boundary stalled near the eastern \nCWA for some of this period. That boundary will give some \nembedded disturbances something to focus along. That will bring \nsome moderate precipitation chances through the weekend and \nMonday, with the highest chances over the eastern third of WA \nand ID. Models are not entirely in good agreement on the \nevolution of the precise waves and precipitation potential, so \nthe forecast will continue to be fine-tuned. Models trends \ntoward the low losing influence Tuesday and Wednesday, Some \nembedded t-storms will remain a risk, but the potential is \ninconsistent as the focus has been changing day to day. Expect \nmoderating temperatures into the weekend. /Solveig",
